string("import std/literals.{Fragment, Literal}\n\n// Two tag types in one file — `Safe` and `Raw` live side by side,\n// dispatching to opposite escaping policies through their own\n// `Literal` impls.\n//\n// Both impls exercise the per-fragment Static/Dynamic distinction\n// — a property the Fragment ADT exposes that a plain `fn(s: String)`\n// can't see, since the caller would have already concatenated\n// everything before the call. The same `${user_input}` reaches `Safe`\n// HTML-escaped and `Raw` verbatim.\n/// `Safe\"<p>${input}</p>\"` — Static text is trusted (verbatim);\n/// Dynamic slots are HTML-escaped before concatenation. The impl\n/// sees Static vs Dynamic separately, which is how it tells trusted\n/// template chrome apart from untrusted user input.\npub type Safe\n\n/// HTML-escape a single piece of untrusted text. Static fragments\n/// (literal source text) bypass this — only Dynamic slots flow\n/// through.\nfn escape(s: String): String {\n    String.replace(s, \"&\", \"&amp;\")\n    |> String.replace(\"<\", \"&lt;\")\n    |> String.replace(\">\", \"&gt;\")\n    |> String.replace(\"\\\"\", \"&quot;\")\n}\n\nimpl Literal for Safe {\n    fn from_fragments(fragments: List<Fragment<String>>): String {\n        Iter.reduce(fragments, |acc = \"\", frag| {\n            case frag {\n                .Static(s) -> acc + s\n                .Dynamic(v) -> acc + escape(v)\n            }\n        })\n    }\n}\n\n/// `Raw\"<p>${html}</p>\"` — same shape, opposite policy: everything is\n/// trusted, no escaping. The contrast with `Sane` is the point — same\n/// source, same fragments list, different impl semaftics.\npub type Raw\n\nimpl Literal for Raw {\n    fn from_fragments(fragments: List<Fragment<String>>): String {\n        Iter.reduce(fragments, |acc = \"\", frag| {\n            case frag {\n                .Static(s) -> acc + s\n                .Dynamic(v) -> acc + v\n            }\n        })\n    }\n}\n")
